# SHOW [GLOBAL|SESSION] VARIABLES [LIKE 'pattern'] conformance.
#
# WordPress's Site Health (WP_Debug_Data::get_mysql_var) issues
# `SHOW VARIABLES LIKE '<name>'` and reads the `Value` column. The front-end
# answers it server-side from a fixed table of plausible system-variable values
# (the same table that backs `SELECT @@var`), returning the MySQL-shaped
# `Variable_name` / `Value` result set ordered by name. The `LIKE` pattern uses
# SQL wildcards (`%`, `_`) and is case-insensitive; an unknown variable yields an
# empty result set (so get_mysql_var returns null). A boolean variable's value
# is rendered ON/OFF, as a real mysqld does.
#
# The asserted values are front-end constants chosen to also match a
# WordPress-configured mysqld (autocommit ON, utf8mb4 client charset). Bare
# `SHOW VARIABLES` is intentionally not asserted (a real mysqld returns hundreds
# of rows). This test needs no tables.

# An exact name returns a single Variable_name / Value row.
query
SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'autocommit'
----
autocommit	ON

# The GLOBAL / SESSION scope is accepted (and ignored).
query
SHOW SESSION VARIABLES LIKE 'autocommit'
----
autocommit	ON

query
SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES LIKE 'max_allowed_packet'
----
max_allowed_packet	67108864

# A `%` wildcard can match several variables; rows are ordered by name.
query
SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'character_set_c%'
----
character_set_client	utf8mb4
character_set_connection	utf8mb4

# The match is case-insensitive.
query
SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'AUTOCOMMIT'
----
autocommit	ON

# A `_` wildcard matches a single character.
query
SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'autocommi_'
----
autocommit	ON

# An unknown variable yields an empty result set.
query
SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'no_such_variable_xyzzy'
----
